@startuml proto-sr04-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-sr04-timing.puml - (c) HC-SR04 timing, and exactly which numbers are
' measured.
'
' From tb/tb_pe_soc_sr04.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real SRAM macro):
'
'   image: 387 words loaded from firmware/sr04_range.hex (637 NOP-filled)
'   run 0: echo 1160 us -> firmware 1160 us, answer 199 mm (expected 199 mm)
'   run 1: echo 5816 us -> firmware 5816 us, answer 999 mm (expected 999 mm)
'   run 2: echo 2000 us -> firmware 2000 us, answer 343 mm (expected 343 mm)
'   run 3: echo 8000 us -> firmware 8000 us, answer 1375 mm (expected 1375 mm)
'   PASS: all checks
' ===========================================================================

title HC-SR04 - timing, all four runs measured\n<font size=12>trigger 601 clocks = 10.0167 us, pinned as an equality; 199 / 999 / 343 / 1375 mm</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "TRIG\npin 6" as TRIG
participant "the sensor\nmodel" as SENSE
participant "ECHO\npin 5, read back" as ECHO
participant "the 1 us counter" as TICK

== the trigger: 601 clocks, pinned as an equality ==

CPU -> TRIG : TXPIN = SR_TRIG, PINOE = SR_TRIG
note over TRIG
  <b>4 * SR_TRIG_LEN + 5 = 4 * 149 + 5 = 601 clocks
  = 10.0167 us</b>

  The device asks for 10 us MINIMUM, so the extra 17 ns is
  harmless to a real sensor - and the check is an EQUALITY
  anyway, because the act claims the COUNT and not a
  datasheet floor. That is the same discipline
  firmware/ws2812.pe uses when a 74-clock cell clears
  every window in print.
end note
note over TRIG
  <b>SR_TRIG_LEN (149), NOT SR_TRIG (0x40).</b>
  ...
  SR_TRIG      = 0x40  the PIN BIT - which pad drives the trigger
  SR_TRIG_LEN  =  149  the DELAY COUNT - how long it is held
  SR_ECHO      = 0x20  the echo pin bit, released and read back
  SR_RECOV_LEN =  117  the sensor's recovery wait
  ...
  Both SR_TRIG and SR_TRIG_LEN exist in peasm's CONSTS, which is
  exactly why naming the wrong one is easy.
end note
CPU -> TRIG : PINOE = 0x00 - release
SENSE -> ECHO : recovery, then 8 x 40 kHz, then ECHO HIGH
note over ECHO
  <b>HIGH FOR THE ROUND TRIP, and the firmware does not
  look at the 8 bursts</b> - it cannot resynchronise to a burst
  it did not send.
  ...
  <b>THE ANSWER IS THE WIDTH, NOT A LEVEL,</b> which is what
  makes the act safe at 4 m: a width does not care when you
  looked at it, where a polled level sample adds its own
  uncertainty to every reading.
end note

== measure: the counter as a difference ==

CPU -> ECHO : spin until the echo goes HIGH
CPU -> TICK : read the COUNTER, not the flag
note over TICK
  <b>THE 1 us COUNTER, read as a DIFFERENCE</b> - exactly as in
  firmware/freqmeter.pe, for the same reason: the clear-on-read
  flag on port 6 is ONE BIT, so a poll loop longer than a tick
  reports several ticks as one, and the loss is invisible in
  the result.
  ...
  <b>Port 7 is the 4.33 us UART half-bit tick and is not a
  microsecond.</b>
end note
TICK -> CPU : now
CPU -> CPU : t = what accumulated, the counter is RESET at
CPU -> CPU :   the rising edge and READ at the falling edge
note over CPU
  <b>ONE RESET REMOVES BOTH THE 16-BIT SUBTRACT AND THE
  COMPARE.</b> This machine has no carry flag, so the borrow of
  a-b cannot be read off the difference - (a-b) mod 256 has two
  preimages, one positive and one negative, for every value
  except zero - which needs a four-case analysis on bit 7, and
  a COMPARE is the same analysis again. Measured rather than
  subtracted, neither appears.
end note

== convert, per run ==

group run 0 - a small target
  SENSE -> ECHO : 1 160 us
  CPU -> CPU : t = 1 160 us
  CPU -> CPU : mm = 1160 * 11/64 = 199.375 -> 199 mm
  note over CPU
    <b>EXPECTED 199 mm. The product is exact; 199 is its
    TRUNCATION.</b>
  end note
end group

group run 1 - the ten-bit path AND the high byte
  SENSE -> ECHO : 5 816 us
  CPU -> CPU : mm = 5816 * 11/64 = 999.4375 -> 999 mm
  note over CPU
    <b>EXPECTED 999 mm.</b> This is the target that used to
    FAIL - the act's last red state was "measurement 1: 1160 us
    is X mm" - so it is the run the fix was for.
    ...
    It exercises the case an overflow would hide: the answer
    would still be a plausible millimetre figure.
  end note
end group

group run 2 - a mid target
  SENSE -> ECHO : 2 000 us
  CPU -> CPU : mm = 2000 * 11/64 = 343.75 -> 343 mm
  note over CPU
    <b>EXPECTED 343 mm.</b>
  end note
end group

group run 3 - the case that ISOLATES the big term
  SENSE -> ECHO : 8 000 us
  CPU -> CPU : mm = 8000 * 11/64 = 1375 EXACTLY -> 1375 mm
  note over CPU
    <b>EXPECTED 1375 mm, and nothing was truncated.</b>
    ...
    8 000 = 64 * 125, so r = 0 and floor() is the identity.
    ...
    <b>AND THAT MAKES IT BLIND TO THE SMALL TERM, NOT SHARPER FOR IT</b> - the
    opposite of what an earlier revision of this figure claimed. With the small
    term forced to 0: 1160 -> 198 WRONG, 5816 -> 990 WRONG, 2000 -> 341 WRONG,
    and 8000 still -> 1375. The small term contributes 1, 9, 2 and <b>0</b>.
    ...
    <b>So run 3 does not test the small term at all - it ISOLATES 11*Q,</b> and
    that is what earns it its place. Two further reasons, and both are the
    OPPOSITE direction to blindness - they are where this run is SHARP:
    ...
    * the conversion masks the high byte of us with 0x3F, and those high bytes
      are 0x04, 0x16, 0x07 and 0x1F. <b>Bit 3 is set in 0x1F ALONE</b>, so a
      slip in that mask (mutation sr-q-mask) is invisible in three runs and
      caught only here: Q falls 125 -> 93, the answer 1375 -> 1023.
    * <b>it is the only width whose us x 11 OVERFLOWS 16 bits</b>: 8 000 x 11 =
      88 000 against a 65 535 ceiling. The nearest miss is 5 816 x 11 = 63 976,
      1 559 short - so 5 816 and 8 000 BRACKET the boundary rather than sitting
      on one side of it.
  end note
end group

== the timing table ==

note over CPU, ECHO
  |= quantity |= required | produced | which side | status |=
  | trigger pulse | >= 10 us; the act derives 4*SR_TRIG_LEN+5 = 601 clocks | <b>601 clocks = 10.0167 us</b> | host | PASS, an equality |
  | echo 1 | 1 160 us | <b>1 160 us</b> | sensor | PASS, exact |
  | echo 2 | 5 816 us | <b>5 816 us</b> | sensor | PASS, exact |
  | echo 3 | 2 000 us | <b>2 000 us</b> | sensor | PASS, exact |
  | echo 4 | 8 000 us | <b>8 000 us</b> | sensor | PASS, exact |
  | mm from echo 1 | 1160 x 11/64 = 199.375 | <b>199 mm</b> | host | PASS, truncated exactly |
  | mm from echo 2 | 5816 x 11/64 = 999.4375 | <b>999 mm</b> | host | PASS, truncated exactly |
  | mm from echo 3 | 2000 x 11/64 = 343.75 | <b>343 mm</b> | host | PASS, truncated exactly |
  | mm from echo 4 | 8000 x 11/64 = 1375 | <b>1375 mm</b> | host | PASS - no truncation, r = 0, and the only 16-bit overflow |
  | runs banked | 4 | <b>4</b> | host | PASS |
  | mutation cases | 2 of 61 distinct MERGED | <b>sr-trig-count, sr-q-mask</b> | both | PASS, both detected |
  | the firmware image | 1 024 words of imem | <b>387 words, 637 NOP-filled</b> | host | PASS - see below |
  ...
  <b>AND NOTE WHAT IS NOT IN THE TABLE.</b> No row is a failure, and no row is a
  tolerance. The one number that could have been a band - the 11/64 constant's own
  0.22 % against the speed of sound - is deliberately NOT here: it belongs to the
  constant and not to the arithmetic, and folding it into a measured row would make
  two different claims look like one number.
end note

== the image: 387 words of 1024 ==

note over CPU
  <b>"387 WORDS VS 1024 REQUESTED" READS AS A DEFECT AND IS NOT ONE.</b>
  ...
  * the imem is 1 024 words (IMEM_WORDS in the TB, pe_soc.v);
  ...
  * <b>tools/fw/peasm.py pads the image to the requested width with 0xF000</b>, which
    is NOP, and says so in the generated file's own comment;
  ...
  * <b>$readmemh into [0:1023] from a 387-word file leaves 637 words unwritten</b>
    and reports it only in a WARNING - so the testbench now counts the file's own
    length, asks $readmemh for exactly that many words, and pre-fills the array with
    16'hF000 before reading;
  ...
  * the firmware <b>parks at halt: JMP halt</b>, so the fill is a no-op region behind a
    program that never returns there.
  ...
  The earlier symptom-only narration - an image that looked short - was a consequence
  of the testbench asking $readmemh for more words than the file held. The length is
  counted now, which is why the number is PRINTED rather than inferred from a warning.
end note

== what this figure used to claim, and why the correction moved with the page ==

note over CPU, ECHO
  An earlier revision of this figure quoted the act's failing output verbatim, the way
  a page quoting a defect has to:
  ...
  '   FAIL the trigger pulse is EXACTLY 600 clocks = 10.000 us (measured 601 = 10.017 us)
  ...
  and wiki/concepts/protocol-sr04.md carried the same quotation with a SIC marker,
  while <b>tools/diag/delay_lattice.py carried a SIC EXEMPTION justified by it</b>.
  Those had to move TOGETHER, and they did.
  ...
  <b>WHY THEY HAD TO:</b> the marker is a self-limiting exemption by construction. A
  marker on a conversion that now recomputes CORRECTLY is itself reported as a
  finding, and a marker on a line with no conversion is reported as noise. So the
  moment the quoted defect was fixed upstream, the marker's justification expired with
  it - and a marker outliving the defect it marked would have switched off a real
  check on a line that had become correct.
  ...
  <b>AND THE QUOTATION ITSELF HAD THREE ERRORS IN IT:</b> the count was 601 not 600,
  the derivation was +5 not +4, and the constant was SR_TRIG_LEN not SR_TRIG. The
  unit error - 600 clocks reported as 10000.000 us, out by 1000 - was a fourth, and it was
  fixed upstream in f7ac41b by adding CLK_US = CLK_NS / 1000.0.
  ...
  <b>The four targets are why the wrong derivation could not survive contact:</b> a
  figure claiming 600 would be checked against a testbench that pins 601, and the
  8 000 us run - the one with no truncation - is what makes the two senses of "exact"
  separable on the page rather than a matter of opinion.
end note

@enduml
